    "examples": [
      {
        "question_text": "Every side of this square is $6$ cm and every angle is a right angle.\n[tikz]\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.75,every node/.style={font=\\normalsize,text=black}]\n\\path[use as bounding box] (-0.2,-0.2) rectangle (3.2,3.2);\n\\draw[black] (0,0) rectangle (3,3);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n[/tikz]\n(a) Classify it as regular or irregular. Give both checks.\n(b) Does a polygon need right angles to be regular?",
        "solution_text": "(a) All four sides are equal and all four angles are equal. The square is regular.\n(b) No. It needs equal angles, not necessarily right angles. An equilateral triangle is regular and has three equal angles of $60^{\\circ}$."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Classify each quadrilateral as regular or irregular. Explain which check decides.\n(a) A rhombus has four sides of $8$ cm, two angles of $60^{\\circ}$ and two of $120^{\\circ}$.\n[tikz]\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.75,every node/.style={font=\\normalsize,text=black}]\n\\draw[black] (0,-1.5)--(2.598076,0)--(0,1.5)--(-2.598076,0)--cycle;\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n[/tikz]\n(b) A rectangle has side lengths $9$ cm, $5$ cm, $9$ cm and $5$ cm, and four right angles.\n[tikz]\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.75,every node/.style={font=\\normalsize,text=black}]\n\\path[use as bounding box] (-0.2,-0.2) rectangle (4.7,2.7);\n\\draw[black] (0,0) rectangle (4.5,2.5);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n[/tikz]",
        "solution_text": "(a) Its sides are all equal, but its angles are not all equal. The rhombus is irregular.\n(b) Its angles are all equal, but its sides are not all equal. The rectangle is irregular."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "(a) A pentagon has five sides of $7$ cm. What else must be checked before calling it regular?\n(b) An octagon has seven sides of $4$ cm and one side of $6$ cm. Must every angle be measured to classify it?\n(c) Mia says equal sides alone always make a polygon regular. Give a quadrilateral counterexample.",
        "solution_text": "(a) Check that all five angles are equal as well.\n(b) No. Its sides are not all equal, so it is already known to be irregular.\n(c) A non-square rhombus has four equal sides but its angles are not all equal. It is irregular, so Mia's claim is false."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Leo says, ‘If all the angles of a polygon are equal, it must be regular.’ Give a quadrilateral counterexample and explain why it disproves his claim.",
        "solution_text": "A rectangle with side lengths $10$ cm, $6$ cm, $10$ cm and $6$ cm has four equal right angles.\n\n[tikz]\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=4mm,y=4mm,every node/.style={font=\\fontsize{10}{12}\\selectfont,text=black}]\n\\draw[black] (0,0)--(10,0)--(10,6)--(0,6)--cycle;\n\\draw[black] (0,0.5)--(0.5,0.5)--(0.5,0);\n\\draw[black] (9.5,0)--(9.5,0.5)--(10,0.5);\n\\draw[black] (10,5.5)--(9.5,5.5)--(9.5,6);\n\\draw[black] (0.5,6)--(0.5,5.5)--(0,5.5);\n\\path (5,0) node[below] {$10\\text{ cm}$};\n\\path (10,3) node[right] {$6\\text{ cm}$};\n\\path (5,6) node[above] {$10\\text{ cm}$};\n\\path (0,3) node[left] {$6\\text{ cm}$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n[/tikz]\n\nIts sides are not all equal, so it is irregular. Equal angles alone do not make a polygon regular."
      }
    ],
